---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MediaCredit from "../components/MediaCredit.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { asset } from "../data/paths";
---

<Layout title={`Accessibility & media credits — ${site.wordmark}`}>
  <div class="reading-container">
    <header class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Access & attribution</p><h1>
        Accessibility & media credits
      </h1><p class="lead">The design target is WCAG 2.1 Level A and AA.</p><p>
        This author-maintained research site uses semantic HTML, a neutral
        palette, keyboard-operable controls, and descriptive alternatives for
        research media. Automated checks are part of the deployment workflow;
        they do not establish complete ADA or WCAG conformance.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <section>
        <h2>Using this site</h2><ul>
          <li>
            The first keyboard link skips to the main content. Keyboard focus is
            visible throughout the page.
          </li><li>
            Silent videos play and loop when visible unless reduced motion or
            data saving is enabled. Each video’s native controls allow you to
            pause or resume playback. Manual pauses are remembered when
            scrolling, and offscreen videos pause automatically.
          </li><li>
            Short field and laboratory clips are distributed across the page.
            Each retains its original speed, has no audio track, and includes a
            complete text alternative and timed WebVTT descriptions.
          </li><li>
            The title and author names appear above the complete video frames.
            Larger screens show the two title videos side by side; phones stack
            them. The footage is not cropped or covered by title text. Native
            fullscreen controls remain available.
          </li><li>
            Laboratory clips show selected movements from independent
            recordings. They preserve the safety tether and operator in the
            original frame and end before later falls or resets. Each
            description identifies the source and visible action; these clips do
            not show complete trial outcomes or independent roofing work.
          </li><li>
            Paper figures have descriptive alternatives and longer explanations.
            The page presents the method and observed movements visually; the
            original paper contains the quantitative evaluation.
          </li><li>
            At narrow widths, content reflows into a single column. BibTeX text
            scrolls within its own focusable area.
          </li><li>
            Core content, native video controls, and full text alternatives
            remain available without JavaScript.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <section id="attachments">
        <h2>Paper & figure accessibility</h2><p>
          This website presents selected content from the <a
            href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.20558">roofing paper on arXiv</a
          >. Descriptions accompany the displayed figures and videos. For the
          complete study, arXiv also provides an <a href={site.htmlPaper}
            >experimental HTML rendering of the preprint</a
          >.
        </p><p>
          The supplied manuscript PDF is untagged and remains unchanged. We have
          not verified the external PDF’s tags, reading order, mathematical
          accessibility, or assistive-technology behavior. The HTML rendering is
          an additional reading option, not a certified accessible version.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Checks and remaining review</h2><p>
          The repository includes an axe-core check against WCAG 2.1 A/AA rules,
          plus checks for keyboard navigation, media behavior, image
          alternatives, 320 CSS-pixel reflow, text spacing, and content with
          JavaScript disabled. A failing check blocks automatic deployment. See <a
            href={`${site.siteRepo}/actions`}>the deployment checks</a
          > and <a href={`${site.siteRepo}/blob/main/ACCESSIBILITY.md`}
            >the review record</a
          >.
        </p><p>
          The current verification record covers motion preferences, autoplay,
          pause persistence, media alternatives, and the responsive page layout.
          Recorded automated results apply only to the revision tested.
        </p><p>
          Automated coverage is limited. A full review with multiple screen
          readers, users with disabilities, and accessibility specialists
          remains outside the completed technical checks. This page does not
          claim an ADA or WCAG certification.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Report an access barrier</h2><p>
          Email <a href="mailto:liusongyang@ufl.edu"
            >Songyang Liu at liusongyang@ufl.edu</a
          >. Please include the page, the content you need, and the browser or
          assistive technology involved so we can help provide a usable format.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Privacy</h2><p>
          This static site loads its fonts, figures, and research-team video
          clips locally. It uses no analytics, advertising, or embedded
          third-party players. Following an external link opens the provider’s
          site, where that provider’s privacy practices apply.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <div class="section"><MediaCredit /></div>
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Research figure credits</h2><p>
        Selected figures come from the user-supplied arXiv preprint by {
          site.authors.map((a) => a.name).join(", ")
        }. Figure numbers and descriptive explanations accompany the figures on
        the <a href={asset()}>project page</a>. Original third-party credits
        remain in the source paper. Rights in research papers and third-party
        media remain with their respective holders.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
